<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Weingarten, Württemberg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Weingarten is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-swabia">Upper Swabia</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, just north of Ravensburg; it was called Altdorf until 1865. It grew around Weingarten Abbey, the family monastery of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Welf dynasty</a>, which kept a relic of the Holy Blood and became an imperial abbey. Its great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> basilica, consecrated in 1724, is one of the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/weingarten-wurttemberg.jpg" alt="Weingarten, Württemberg" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo: Andreas Praefcke · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Weingarten_Basilika_Fassade_Abt-Hyller-Strasse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Weingarten lies in the valley of the Schussen River in the District of Ravensburg, with Ravensburg to the south and Friedrichshafen on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>. Before 1865 the town was called Altdorf, from the Frankish alach for church, so that the name means a village with the parish church; Weingarten was then the name of the abbey alone, which stood on the Martinsberg above the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>An Alemannic burial place of the 5th century was excavated near the old town in 1954 to 1957. In the 8th century the region became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Frankish empire</a>, and around the 9th century the Elder Welfs became counts of the Schussengau and made Altdorf their seat. In 1056 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/welf-i-duke-of-bavaria">Welf IV</a> moved the ancestral seat to the new castle of Ravensburg and founded a Benedictine abbey on the Martinsberg, which was named Weingarten Abbey. In 1191 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> acquired the Schussengau from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/welf-vi">Welf VI</a> by a contract of inheritance. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> line ended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conradin">Conradin</a>'s death in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in 1268, the abbey won the status of an Imperial Abbey, while Altdorf was ruled by the imperial bailiff of Swabia.</p><p>The abbey became one of the wealthiest monasteries in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, with about 306 km2 of estates, until it was confiscated in the secularisation of 1803. Weingarten went first to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-nassau">House of Nassau</a> and in 1806 was incorporated into Württemberg, while Altdorf had passed to the dukedom of Württemberg. Barracks made it a military site in the 19th century, and an engineering industry developed in the second half of the century. In 1922 monks from Beuron founded a new Benedictine abbey. Since 1949 most of the abbey buildings have housed a teachers' college.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Abbey Church of St. Martin and Oswald is the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> church north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, about half as long as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-peters-basilica">St. Peter's Basilica</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Its organ by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-gabler">Joseph Gabler</a> has four manuals and nearly 7,000 pipes. The relic of the holy blood of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus Christ</a> is carried in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blutritt">Blutritt</a>, a large equestrian procession of about 3,000 riders held on the Friday after Ascension. The Alemans Museum displays finds from the burial place, and the Schlössle, built around 1550 for the imperial bailiff, houses the municipal museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Weingarten, Württemberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Guelph family founded a convent at <b>Altdorf</b> near Ravensburg in the 11th century and in 1056 replaced it with a Benedictine abbey that became their family monastery.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-weingarten">Imperial Abbey of Weingarten</a></li></ul></section>
